You are an expert Python programmer, and here is your task: Write a function to add the given list to the given tuples. Your code should pass these tests:

assert add_lists([5, 6, 7], (9, 10)) == (9, 10, 5, 6, 7)
assert add_lists([6, 7, 8], (10, 11)) == (10, 11, 6, 7, 8)
assert add_lists([7, 8, 9], (11, 12)) == (11, 12, 7, 8, 9)
[BEGIN]

answer:  def add_lists(lst, tup):
    # Convert the tuple to a list
    tup_list = list(tup)
    
    # Add the list to the tuple
    tup_list.extend(lst)
    
    # Convert the list back to a tuple
    return tuple(tup_list)

# Test cases
assert add_lists([5, 6, 7], (9, 10)) == (9, 10, 5, 6, 7)
assert add_lists([6, 7, 8], (10, 11)) == (10, 11, 6, 7, 8)
assert add_lists([7, 8, 9], (11, 12)) == (11, 12, 7, 8, 9)

print("All tests passed!")

This function `add_lists` takes a list and a tuple as input, converts the tuple to a list, adds the list to the tuple, and then converts the list back to a tuple. The test cases provided at the end ensure that the function produces the expected results.
